### Basic Nixvim Configuration

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/README.md

A minimal configuration enabling the catppuccin colorscheme and lualine plugin.

```nix
{
  programs.nixvim = {
    enable = true;

    colorschemes.catppuccin.enable = true;
    plugins.lualine.enable = true;
  };
}
```

--------------------------------

### Enable a Nixvim plugin

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/README.md

Enable a supported plugin by setting its module enable option to true.

```nix
{
  programs.nixvim = {
    plugins.lightline.enable = true;
  };
}
```

--------------------------------

### Register and Configure Extra Nixvim Plugins

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/docs/user-guide/faq.md

Use `extraPlugins` to register plugins packaged in nixpkgs and `extraConfigLua` to configure them.

```nix
extraPlugins = [pkgs.vimPlugins."<plugin name>"]
extraConfigLua = "require('my-plugin').setup({foo = \"bar\"})";
```

--------------------------------

### Add extra plugins

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/README.md

Use extraPlugins to include plugins that do not have a dedicated Nixvim module.

```nix
{
  programs.nixvim = {
    extraPlugins = with pkgs.vimPlugins; [
      vim-nix
    ];
  };
}
```

--------------------------------

### Importing Nixvim Modules

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/docs/user-guide/install.md

Add Nixvim modules to your configuration for NixOS, Home Manager, or nix-darwin. This enables Nixvim and allows configuration via `programs.nixvim`.

```nix
# home-config.nix
{
  # Imported modules are scoped within the `programs.nixvim` submodule
  programs.nixvim.imports = [ ./nixvim.nix ];
}

```

```nix
# nixvim.nix
{
  # You can use lib.nixvim in your config
  fooOption = lib.nixvim.mkRaw "print('hello')";

  # Configure Nixvim without prefixing with `plugins.nixvim`
  plugins.my-plugin.enable = true;
}

```
<dcp-message-id>m0006</dcp-message-id>

### Configure nixd with Nixvim Options

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/docs/platforms/standalone.md

Access Nixvim module options to configure external tools like nixd. This example shows how to provide Nixvim's options expression to nixd.

```nix
{
  plugins.lsp.servers.nixd = {
    enable = true;

    settings.options.nixvim.expr =
      ''(builtins.getFlake "/path/to/flake").packages.${system}.default.options'';
  };
}
```

### Custom LSP server modules

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/modules/lsp/servers/custom/README.md

Modules related to specific LSP servers are located in this directory. Files are automatically imported into their respective submodules based on their filename. For instance, `foo.nix` or `foo/default.nix` would be imported into the `lsp.servers.foo` submodule. A corresponding `lsp.servers.<name>` option must be defined for each module in this directory.
<dcp-message-id>m0007</dcp-message-id>

### fromLua loader config generation

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/plugins/by-name/luasnip/default.nix

Shows how the `fromLua` (and fromVscode/fromSnipmate) loaders are converted into Lua `require(...).load(...)` calls at config time, so `paths` from the fromLua option become the paths argument passed to the luasnip lua loader.

```nix
            (map (
              pair:
              let
                inherit (pair) name loader;
                options = lib.getAttrs [
                  "paths"
                  "exclude"
                  "include"
                ] loader;
              in
              ''
                require("luasnip.loaders.from_${name}").${lib.optionalString loader.lazyLoad "lazy_"}load(${toLuaObject options})
              ''
            ))
```

--------------------------------

### fromLua option with paths example

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/plugins/by-name/luasnip/default.nix

Defines the `plugins.luasnip.fromLua` option, which loads Lua snippets via the luasnip lua loader. The example shows how to specify snippet paths: a single path `./path/to/snippets` or an empty loader `{}` for the default runtimepath.

```nix
    fromLua = mkOption {
      default = [ ];
      description = ''
        Load lua snippets with the lua loader.
        Check <https://github.com/L3MON4D3/LuaSnip/blob/master/DOC.md#lua> for the necessary file structure.
      '';
      example = lib.literalExpression ''
        [
          {}
          {
            paths = ./path/to/snippets;
          }
        ]
      '';
      type = types.listOf loaderSubmodule;
    };
```
<dcp-message-id>m0007</dcp-message-id>

### Define Key Mappings

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/README.md

Define key mappings using the keymaps attribute, which accepts a list of mapping objects.

```nix
{
  programs.nixvim = {
    keymaps = [
      {
        key = ";";
        action = ":";
      }
      {
        mode = "n";
        key = "<leader>m";
        options.silent = true;
        action = "<cmd>!make<CR>";
      }
    ];
  };
}
```

```vim
noremap ; :
nnoremap <leader>m <silent> <cmd>make<CR>
```

--------------------------------

### Generate Multiple Keymap Aliases

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/docs/user-guide/faq.md

Create multiple keymap entries with the same action and description by mapping over a list of keys using the built-in `map` function.

```nix
{
  keymaps =
    (map (key: {
      inherit key;
      action = "<some-action>";
      options.desc = "My cool keymapping";
    }) ["<key-1>" "<key-2>" "<key-3>"])
    ++ [
      # Other keymaps...
    ];
}
```

```nix
{
  keymaps = [
    {
      key = "<key-1>";
      action = "<some-action>";
      options.desc = "My cool keymapping";
    }
    {
      key = "<key-2>";
      action = "<some-action>";
      options.desc = "My cool keymapping";
    }
    {
      key = "<key-3>";
      action = "<some-action>";
      options.desc = "My cool keymapping";
    }
    # Other keymaps...
  ];
}
```

--------------------------------

### Configure Plugin Lazy Loading with Multiple Triggers

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/docs/user-guide/lazy-loading.md

Enable lazy loading for a plugin and specify multiple trigger conditions, such as a command and a set of keymaps. Ensure the plugin is enabled.

```nix
{
  plugins.toggleterm = {
    enable = true;
    lazyLoad.settings = {
      cmd = "ToggleTerm";
      keys = [
        "<leader>tg"
        "<leader>gg"
      ];
    };
  };
}
```

--------------------------------

### Configure Plugin Lazy Loading with Keymaps and Dependency

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/docs/user-guide/lazy-loading.md

Enable lazy loading for a plugin, specifying keymaps as triggers. The `before.__raw` field is used to ensure a dependency (like nvim-dap) is loaded before the plugin's `after` function executes. This is useful when the plugin's functionality relies on another plugin being available.

```nix
{
  plugins.dap-ui = {
    enable = true;
    lazyLoad.settings = {
      # We need to access nvim-dap in the after function.
      before.__raw = ''
        function()
          require('lz.n').trigger_load('nvim-dap')
        end
      '';
      keys = [
        {
          __unkeyed-1 = "<leader>du";
          __unkeyed-2.__raw = ''
            function()
              require('dap.ext.vscode').load_launchjs(nil, {}) 
              require("dapui").toggle()
            end
          '';
          desc = "Toggle Debugger UI";
        }
      ];
    };
  };
}
```

### Key mappings

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/README.md

Key mappings are defined using the keymaps attribute, which accepts a list of mapping configurations. You can specify the mode, the key sequence, the action to perform, and additional options such as silent execution or descriptions. Multiple modes can be assigned to a single mapping by providing a list of strings.
<dcp-message-id>m0007</dcp-message-id>

### Inject custom Lua configuration

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/README.md

Use extraConfigLua to provide custom Lua scripts that are not natively supported by Nixvim options.

```nix
{
  programs.nixvim = {
    extraConfigLua = ''
      -- Print a little welcome message when Neovim is opened!
      print("Hello world!")
    '';
  };
}
```

--------------------------------

### Register and Configure Extra Nixvim Plugins

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/docs/user-guide/faq.md

Use `extraPlugins` to register plugins packaged in nixpkgs and `extraConfigLua` to configure them.

```nix
extraPlugins = [pkgs.vimPlugins."<plugin name>"]
extraConfigLua = "require('my-plugin').setup({foo = \"bar\"})";
```

--------------------------------

### Extend Nixvim Configuration with Modules

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/docs/platforms/standalone.md

Extend an existing Nixvim configuration by layering additional modules. This is useful for progressive configuration.

```nix
let
  base = nixvim.lib.evalNixvim {
    inherit system;

    modules = [ 
      {
        extraConfigLua = "-- first stage";
      }
    ];
  };

  extended = base.extendModules {
    modules = [ 
      {
        extraConfigLua = "-- second stage";
      }
    ];
  };
in
extended.config.build.package
```

### Nixvim > How does it work? > Raw lua

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/README.md

Users can inject custom Lua code into their configuration using specific options like extraConfigLua. Additionally, the raw type feature allows assigning arbitrary Lua code to options by using the __raw attribute.

--------------------------------

### Nixvim > Additional config

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/README.md

For customizations not natively supported by Nixvim, users can utilize the extraConfigVim and extraConfigLua options to inject custom Vim script or Lua code directly. Users are encouraged to open an issue if they believe a manual configuration should be natively supported by the project.
