### Add extra plugins

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/README.md

Use extraPlugins to include plugins that do not have a dedicated Nixvim module.

```nix
{
  programs.nixvim = {
    extraPlugins = with pkgs.vimPlugins; [
      vim-nix
    ];
  };
}
```

--------------------------------

### Enable a Nixvim plugin

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/README.md

Enable a supported plugin by setting its module enable option to true.

```nix
{
  programs.nixvim = {
    plugins.lightline.enable = true;
  };
}
```

--------------------------------

### Scaffold a new plugin

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md

Generates the necessary module and test files for a new plugin using the development shell.

```bash
nix develop --command new-plugin <plugin-name> --package <vimPlugins-package> --maintainer <maintainer>
```

### Plugins

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/README.md

Nixvim utilizes a module-based system for plugins, similar to NixOS and Home Manager. Users can enable supported plugins by setting the corresponding module to true. For plugins without dedicated modules, the extraPlugins option allows users to include additional packages.

--------------------------------

### Functionality

Source: https://github.com/nix-community/nixvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md

The mkVimPlugin function is used to generate a Nix module for plugins. It handles the definition of plugin metadata such as maintainers, descriptions, and URLs, while also establishing options for enabling the plugin, selecting packages, and managing configuration settings.
